(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["physiology-exam-3-immunology-quiz-version-4"]={"title":"Blood & Immunology — Quiz 3 (30 Questions)","category":"Physiology","sem":"summer-1-2026","questions":[{"q":"Antigen fragments presented by MHC I molecules are specifically recognized by...","choices":["natural killer cells exclusively","helper T-cells","B-lymphocytes exclusively","cytotoxic T-cells"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. MHC class I molecules present antigen fragments to cytotoxic T-cells, allowing them to identify and destroy infected or abnormal cells displaying those fragments, while MHC class II instead presents to helper T-cells. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 55"},{"q":"MHC II molecules, in contrast to MHC I, present antigen fragments specifically to...","choices":["cytotoxic T-cells","helper T-cells","red blood cells","neutrophils"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. MHC class II molecules on antigen-presenting cells display antigen fragments to helper T-cells, which then coordinate and amplify the broader immune response; MHC I instead presents to cytotoxic T-cells. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 55"},{"q":"Dendritic cells, macrophages, and B-lymphocytes are all capable antigen-presenting cells, but dendritic cells are considered the most potent because they are...","choices":["the only antigen-presenting cell type that expresses any MHC molecules at all","restricted to the bone marrow cavity only, never leaving it","located throughout tissues, positioned to efficiently capture and present antigen","incapable of migrating to lymphoid organs once they are formed"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Dendritic cells are distributed widely throughout the body's tissues, giving them an especially efficient positioning to encounter, capture, and present antigen to T-cells, making them the most potent of the antigen-presenting cell types. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 55"},{"q":"Cytotoxic (killer) T-cells destroy their target cells by secreting hole-forming proteins called...","choices":["perforins","interferons","thrombosthenin","opsonins"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Killer T-cells secrete perforins, which form pores in the target cell's membrane, allowing cytotoxic substances to be released directly into the attacked cell — after which the killer T-cell can detach and move on to kill additional target cells. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 45"},{"q":"After a killer T-cell releases perforins and cytotoxic substances into one target cell, it characteristically...","choices":["is itself destroyed along with the target cell every time","detaches and moves on to kill additional target cells","becomes permanently inactivated after one kill","converts into a helper T-cell"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Killer T-cells are not consumed in a single cytotoxic event; after destroying one target, they can detach and go on to kill additional cells bearing the same recognized antigen. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 45"},{"q":"Kupffer cells lining the liver sinusoids are so effective at filtering portal blood that they remove approximately what fraction of bacteria in a single pass?","choices":["Essentially 0%, since Kupffer cells mainly process senescent red cells, not bacteria","About 10%","About 50%","About 99%"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Kupffer cells lining the liver sinusoids remove approximately 99% of bacteria present in portal blood in a single pass, making the liver an extremely effective filter given that portal blood carries organisms absorbed from the gut. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 23"},{"q":"Within the spleen, macrophages residing specifically in the red pulp are important for removing...","choices":["invading organisms, debris, and senescent blood cells and platelets","excess circulating glucose during states of hyperglycemia","excess bile salts recycled back from the terminal ileum","only dietary lipids freshly absorbed from the small intestine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages in the red pulp of the spleen (along with those in lymph node sinuses) are richly exposed to circulating blood and are important for clearing invading organisms, cellular debris, and aged (senescent) blood cells and platelets. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 24"},{"q":"IgM, the earliest antibody class produced during an immune response, is structurally distinctive because it is composed of five paired heavy and light chains, giving it...","choices":["2 antigen-binding sites, the same as IgG","a single antigen-binding site","4 antigen-binding sites, the same as IgA","10 antigen-binding sites"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. IgM's pentameric structure (five paired heavy and light chains) gives it 10 antigen-binding sites, more than any other immunoglobulin class, making it especially effective at agglutinating antigens early in an immune response. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 51"},{"q":"IgG is the most abundant antibody isotype, accounting for approximately what fraction of all immunoglobulins?","choices":["About 75%","About 95%","About 10%","About 25%"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. IgG accounts for approximately 75% of all circulating immunoglobulins, consistent with its major role in long-term humoral immunity, while classes like IgA, IgD, and IgE make up smaller fractions. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 51"},{"q":"Once a monocyte matures in the tissues into a macrophage, its diameter increases substantially — by roughly how much compared to its earlier form, reaching up to 60–80 micrometers?","choices":["About 5-fold","About 20-fold","About 1.5-fold","There is no meaningful size change from monocyte to macrophage"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Macrophages can reach roughly 5-fold the diameter of the monocyte they matured from, growing up to 60–80 micrometers — a size increase that accompanies their greater phagocytic capacity compared with neutrophils. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 13"},{"q":"Helper T-cells coordinate and amplify the immune response largely by secreting lymphokines, a group that includes several interleukins along with...","choices":["renin and aldosterone, the key blood-pressure-regulating hormones","erythropoietin and thrombopoietin, the marrow-stimulating hormones","granulocyte-monocyte colony-stimulating factor (GM-CSF) and interferon-gamma","gastrin and secretin, both classic gastrointestinal hormones"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In addition to interleukins (such as IL-2, IL-3, IL-4, IL-5, IL-6, IL-10, IL-11–13), helper T-cell lymphokines include GM-CSF and interferon-gamma — a broad signaling arsenal that the immune system is described as being 'almost paralyzed' without. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 44"},{"q":"Loss of helper T-cells, as occurs in advanced HIV infection, cripples immune defense so severely largely because helper T-cells...","choices":["directly produce all of the body's circulating antibodies by themselves","secrete the lymphokines that activate and coordinate nearly every other arm of the immune response","are the sole cellular source of all circulating complement proteins made in the liver","are the only immune cells anywhere in the body capable of performing phagocytosis"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Because helper T-cells secrete the lymphokines that activate B-cells, cytotoxic T-cells, and macrophages, their loss leaves the immune system 'almost paralyzed,' unable to mount effective humoral or cell-mediated responses even though other immune cell types are still physically present. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 44"},{"q":"Neutrophils and macrophages share three key motility properties that let them reach a site of infection: diapedesis, ameboid motion, and...","choices":["chemotaxis","degranulation","agglutination","opsonization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Alongside diapedesis (squeezing through capillary walls) and ameboid motion (crawling movement), chemotaxis — directed movement toward a chemical gradient released at the infection site — is the third property that lets neutrophils and macrophages reach an infection. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 13"},{"q":"IgD, one of the five antibody isotypes, is structurally similar to IgG in that it has how many antigen-binding sites?","choices":["6","10","2","4"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Like IgG, IgD has 2 antigen-binding sites — fewer than IgA's 4 or IgM's 10 — though IgD's specific physiological role is less prominent than IgG's role in long-term humoral immunity. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 51"},{"q":"The complement system is described as bridging innate and acquired immunity because it can be activated either by antibodies bound to a pathogen (the classical route of activation) or by...","choices":["erythropoietin signaling pathways activated in the kidney","direct recognition of pathogen surface features, independent of antibody","MHC class I presentation to cytotoxic T-cells exclusively","T-cell receptor engagement with presented antigen exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Complement can be triggered either through antibody-antigen complexes (linking it to acquired/humoral immunity) or through direct recognition of certain pathogen surface features without requiring antibody first, which is why it is considered a bridge between innate and acquired immune defenses. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 15"},{"q":"Acquired (adaptive) immunity is distinguished from innate immunity by the fact that it...","choices":["is present from birth and requires no prior antigen exposure to function specifically against any pathogen","takes time (often weeks) to develop after first exposure to a specific pathogen, but then confers lasting specific protection","provides only broad, nonspecific protection against wide categories of pathogens in general","responds in an identical, immediate fashion to any pathogen encountered, with no delay whatsoever"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Unlike innate immunity, which is immediately available and nonspecific, acquired immunity requires time to develop (often weeks after first exposure to a novel bacterium, virus, or toxin) but then provides highly specific and often long-lasting protection against that particular threat. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 35"},{"q":"Natural killer cells are grouped with the innate rather than acquired immune system chiefly because they...","choices":["are generated exclusively through somatic gene rearrangement of antigen receptors","require weeks of antigen-specific clonal expansion before they can kill a target","produce circulating antibodies against specific antigens the way B-cells do","can recognize and kill abnormal cells without requiring prior sensitization to a specific antigen"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Natural killer cells are classified within innate immunity because, unlike cytotoxic T-cells, they can recognize and destroy virus-infected or tumor cells without first needing to be sensitized to a specific antigen through a slow, antigen-driven maturation process. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 35"},{"q":"The two branches of acquired immunity are humoral immunity, mediated by circulating antibodies, and cell-mediated immunity, mediated by...","choices":["circulating antibodies exclusively, identical to the humoral branch","activated (sensitized) lymphocytes themselves","platelets and clotting factors","complement proteins exclusively"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Cell-mediated immunity is carried out directly by activated lymphocytes (chiefly T-cells) rather than by antibodies circulating freely in the blood, which is the hallmark of the humoral branch instead. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 35"},{"q":"T-lymphocytes are named for the organ in which they mature — the thymus — while B-lymphocytes are named, in birds, for their site of maturation, the...","choices":["Spleen","Thymus, the same as T-cells","Bursa of Fabricius","Liver, in both birds and mammals"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. B-lymphocytes are named for the Bursa of Fabricius, the organ in birds where this lymphocyte class was first found to mature; in mammals, the analogous maturation site is the bone marrow (fittingly, also starting with 'B'). — Blood & Immunology, Slide 42"},{"q":"Neutrophils are able to respond to infection essentially immediately because, unlike monocytes, they are...","choices":["already mature cells when released into circulation","produced only during an active infection, never in a resting state","incapable of phagocytosis until activated by helper T-cells","unable to leave the bloodstream at all"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Neutrophils are released from the bone marrow as fully mature cells ready to fight infection immediately, whereas monocytes are comparatively immature and must first migrate into tissues and mature into macrophages before reaching their full antimicrobial capability. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 13"},{"q":"Digestion of an ingested microbe inside a phagocyte occurs after the phagosome (containing the microbe) fuses with a...","choices":["mitochondrion, which directly oxidizes the microbe","Golgi apparatus, which packages the microbe for exocytosis","nucleus, which incorporates and stores microbial DNA fragments","lysosome, forming a phagolysosome that releases digestive enzymes"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Once a phagocyte engulfs a microbe into a phagosome, that phagosome fuses with a lysosome to form a phagolysosome, in which digestive enzymes and other bactericidal agents break down the ingested organism. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 16"},{"q":"A rise in circulating white blood cell count (leukocytosis) during infection reflects which underlying bone marrow response?","choices":["Decreased production of white blood cells in order to conserve marrow resources","A shift of bone marrow activity entirely toward red blood cell production","Release of stored plasma antibodies rather than new cell production","Increased production and release of white blood cells to meet the heightened demand"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. As a later line of defense during a sustained infection, the bone marrow ramps up production and release of white blood cells, producing the leukocytosis (elevated WBC count) commonly seen with active infection. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 19"},{"q":"A particle is generally more likely to be phagocytized when it has a rough (rather than smooth) surface, largely because...","choices":["surface texture has no effect on phagocytosis likelihood","a rough surface gives phagocytes more to physically grip and attach to","a smooth surface always increases the number of opsonin binding sites","a rough surface chemically neutralizes the phagocyte's enzymes"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Phagocytes rely on physical attachment to a particle's surface before they can engulf it, so a rough, irregular surface provides more purchase for the phagocyte to grip, in contrast to a smooth surface that is harder to attach to and engulf. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 16"},{"q":"Pus that forms at a site of bacterial infection is composed largely of...","choices":["living, fully functional lymphocytes exclusively, with no dead cells","pure bacterial cells only, with no host cellular contribution at all","dead and dying neutrophils, macrophages, and tissue cells, along with fluid","circulating antibodies concentrated at the infection site"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Pus consists mainly of dead and dying neutrophils and macrophages that were consumed in the fight against infection, along with destroyed tissue cells and fluid, reflecting the intensity of the local innate immune battle. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 19"},{"q":"Compared with blood capillaries, lymph capillaries are structurally distinctive in that they are...","choices":["far less permeable overall, admitting only very small dissolved ions","identical in permeability and structure to ordinary blood capillaries","completely impermeable along their length until reaching a lymph node","more permeable, allowing larger molecules and even cells to enter"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Lymph capillaries have a more permeable/porous structure than blood capillaries, with overlapping endothelial cells that act like one-way valves, allowing interstitial fluid, large proteins, and even cells to enter the lymphatic system that blood capillaries would not normally admit. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 8"},{"q":"Because the lymphatic system is responsible for returning interstitial fluid to the blood, blockage of lymphatic drainage (lymphatic obstruction) typically results in...","choices":["increased lymphocyte production in the affected limb","immediate and widespread systemic dehydration throughout the body","a sudden drop in local interstitial tissue pressure","localized tissue swelling (lymphedema) from fluid accumulation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When lymphatic drainage is blocked, interstitial fluid that would normally be returned to the blood via the lymphatics instead accumulates in the tissue, producing localized swelling known as lymphedema. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 8"},{"q":"Tonsils and adenoids are positioned in the throat and pharynx because this location allows them to...","choices":["intercept inhaled and ingested pathogens at an early portal of entry into the body","filter circulating blood directly, in the same manner the spleen does","produce new red blood cells specifically during a respiratory infection","regulate breathing rate directly through brainstem chemoreceptor input"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Positioned at the junction of the respiratory and digestive tracts, tonsils and adenoids are strategically located to encounter and respond to pathogens in inhaled air and swallowed food/water — two of the body's most common early portals of entry. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 9"},{"q":"Antibodies bound to a toxin's active (toxic) site can neutralize it directly by...","choices":["activating complement exclusively, with no direct blocking action","converting the toxin into a harmless antigen fragment via digestion","stimulating the toxin to replicate itself into a series of inert copies","physically covering the toxic site so it can no longer interact with its target"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. When an antibody binds directly over a toxin's active/toxic site, it sterically blocks that site from interacting with its cellular target, neutralizing the toxin's harmful effect without needing complement or a phagocyte. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 52"},{"q":"When antibodies bind and cross-link many copies of a bacterium together, clumping them into a mass, this antibody mechanism is called...","choices":["agglutination","opsonization","degranulation","neutralization"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Agglutination occurs when antibodies with multiple binding sites cross-link several antigen-bearing particles (such as bacteria) into a clump, making them easier for phagocytes to engulf and clear. — Blood & Immunology, Slide 52"},{"q":"In an immediate (type I) allergic reaction, symptoms arise when an allergen binds IgE antibodies already attached to mast cells or basophils, triggering...","choices":["activation of the classical complement pathway exclusively, with no IgE involvement","immediate destruction of the allergen by cytotoxic T-cells","degranulation and release of histamine and other inflammatory mediators","permanent neutralization of the allergen with no further symptoms"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. In a type I allergic reaction, an allergen cross-links IgE antibodies bound to mast cells/basophils, triggering degranulation and the release of histamine and other mediators that produce the characteristic allergic symptoms (e.g., swelling, itching, bronchoconstriction). — Blood & Immunology, Slide 60"}]};
